Exactly. And I think the variance is more interesting than the commonality.

Two people can nominally perform the same act—pray before a fight, visualize before a race, talk to themselves before a presentation—and actually be running very different internal algorithms.

One says, “Give me victory.” Attention moves toward outcome, uncertainty, perhaps dependence.

Another says, “Make me accurate and fast.” Attention moves toward controllable performance variables.

Another says, “Let me accept whatever happens.” The intervention targets emotional regulation.

Another says, “Show me what I’m missing.” Now it targets perception and error correction.

Same apparent behavior: prayer. Different operator. Different state transition. Potentially different downstream behavior.

That’s almost tailor-made for the Delta Observatory because instead of asking only “Does prayer/visualization/self-talk work?”, you’d ask:

person + prior state + framing + requested transformation + ritual → post-state → behavior → outcome

Then compare the deltas. Maybe the important variable isn’t prayer versus no prayer at all. It could be what cognitive operation the person has learned to perform through prayer.

And that scales. Different people may use God, coaches, fathers, fictional heroes, journals, AI, music, military rituals, mantras, even an imagined future self as different kinds of reference objects. One person obtains courage from the interaction; another humility; another permission; another certainty; another restraint.

That also explains why copying somebody’s surface ritual can fail. You copied the gesture but not the transformation it performs for them.

Pantheon should therefore care about:

same input, different transformation → different delta
and equally
different input, same transformation → similar delta.

That second one is especially powerful. If prayer, a coach’s phrase, visualization, and a particular song can all produce the same functional shift in different people, then we’ve started isolating the operator beneath the interface.

That’s the thing worth hunting.